core-init-rc1 regression -- WCD9320 stage split, behaviour-neutral
==================================================================
2026-08-01. Kernel 6.16.12 r129, module wcd9320 core-init-rc1.

Purpose: confirm that splitting core release from RCO clocking behind one
shared executor changed nothing observable. Compared against rco-wake-rc3,
the last build validated on hardware.

Cold boot (full power-off, not a warm reboot), so this exercised the fresh
path rather than adoption.


### start state -- genuinely clean ###

  precondition: sentinel 0x200-0x3bf, 0 of 448 registers non-zero

  The power-off cleared the core left accessible by the previous rc3 run.
  Had this read non-zero, the run would have been exercising the adoption
  path and would not have been comparable.

  identity   major 0x0102 minor 0x0001, laddr 0xcb / IFD 0xca
  masks      written ff ff 3f 7f, read back ff ff 3f 7f (no mismatch)
  irq        83 msmgpio 72 Edge, trigger 0x1, count 0
  samples    45/45, pad high in 0, live source in 0
  RC_OSC     freq=46 test=0a status=18
  BG_CTL 50  CLK_BUFF_EN1 04  CLK_BUFF_EN2 02  TLMM72 low


### the two stages now run separately ###

  === stage 1: releasing the digital core ===
  bring-up step 1/4  0x088 LEAKAGE_CTL  old=04 -> 04
  bring-up step 2/4  0x080 CDC_CTL      old=00 -> 00   (5 ms)
  bring-up step 3/4  0x080 CDC_CTL      old=00 -> 03
  bring-up step 4/4  0x088 LEAKAGE_CTL  old=04 -> 03
  bring-up: all 4 steps applied cleanly

  === stage 2: bandgap and RCO clock block ===
  rco-wake step 1/15  0x101 old=50 mask=80 want=80 -> d0  OK
  rco-wake step 2/15  0x101 old=d0 mask=04 want=04 -> d4  OK
  rco-wake step 3/15  0x101 old=d4 mask=01 want=01 -> d5  OK  (1 ms)
  rco-wake step 4/15  0x101 old=d5 mask=80 want=00 -> 55  OK
  rco-wake step 5/15  0x1fa old=46 mask=10 want=00 -> 46  OK
  rco-wake step 6/15  0x105 old=16 write 0x17      -> 17  OK  (5 us)
  ... through step 15/15

  The bandgap writes take on the first attempt. In rc1 and rc2 these were
  exactly the writes that were silently refused, because the core was still
  in reset. That is the regression this split is meant to keep visible.

  Table sizes: bring-up 4 steps, rco-wake 15. Previously one 19-step table.
  4 + 15 = 19, and the registers, values, order and delays are unchanged.


### result -- identical to rco-wake-rc3 ###

  sentinel non-zero   0 -> 95 of 448        (rc3: 0 -> 95)

  stability, three live re-reads 2 s apart:
    0x320=e4  0x314=00  0x311=01  0x223=48  0x36b=37   every pass
    pass 1 vs 2 byte-identical, pass 2 vs 3 byte-identical


### teardown -- identical to rco-wake-rc3 ###

  rco-sleep step 1/6  0x109 old=04 mask=04 want=00 -> 00  OK  (50 us)
  rco-sleep step 2/6  0x109 old=00 mask=02 want=02 -> 02  OK
  rco-sleep step 3/6  0x108 old=0d mask=05 want=00 -> 08  OK  (50 us)
  rco-sleep step 4/6  0x105 old=17 mask=01 want=00 -> 16  OK
  rco-sleep step 5/6  0x1fa old=c6 mask=80 want=00 -> 46  OK
  rco-sleep step 6/6  0x101 old=55 mask=03 want=00 -> 54  OK  (100 us)
  rco-sleep: all 6 steps applied cleanly

  sentinel non-zero 0 -> 95 -> 95 of 448 (remains accessible)   (rc3: same)


### carried-forward gates, all held ###

  both functions bound            2
  identity                        0x0102 / 0x0001, unchanged throughout
  logical addresses               PGD 0xcb, IFD 0xca
  adsp                            running
  ngd                             1
  slim errors / timeouts          0 / 0
  irq masks / status              ff ff 3f 7f / 00 00 00 00
  parent irq assertions           0, before and after


### one incidental difference, not a regression ###

  The parent IRQ is 83 this boot; it was 87 previously. That is a Linux
  virtual IRQ number, allocated dynamically at request time. The underlying
  pin is still msmgpio 72 and the kernel still reports trigger type 0x1.

  Consequence: irq=87 must not be treated as a fixed expectation in any
  future acceptance check. Assert on the pin and trigger type, not the
  virtual number.


### what this does NOT cover ###

  Still absent by design, and still the next milestone: core_ready, the
  mutex idempotency guard, three-stage snapshots, automatic initialisation
  on the fresh path, and the adoption-path sentinel check. The wake here is
  still triggered manually.

  So this is a behaviour-neutrality regression for the refactor, not
  wcd9320-core-init-proven.
